Certainly! Here's a simple JavaScript function that will switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function swapAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Filter out any words that aren't adjectives (e.g., nouns, verbs, etc.)
  const adjectives = words.filter((word) => word.match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/));

  // Reverse the order of the adjectives
  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();

  // Join the reversed adjectives back into a sentence
  const newSentence = reversedAdjectives.join(" ");

  return newSentence;
}

// Example usage
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const newSentence = swapAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function first splits the input sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then filters out any words that aren't adjectives by using a regular expression that matches one or more alphabetical characters (`[a-zA-Z]+`). Finally, it reverses the order of the adjectives and joins them back into a sentence using the `join()` method. The resulting sentence has the adjectives in the opposite order as the original sentence.

