To achieve this, you can use Google Apps Script along with the Google Maps API. Here's a simple script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A1:C10"); // Assuming your data range is in columns A to C, rows 1 to 10
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue(1, 1); // Get the place name from cell A1

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ address: placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == 'OK') {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      // Store the latitude and longitude in the corresponding columns
      sheet.getRange(1, 3).setValue(latitude);
      sheet.getRange(1, 4).setValue(longitude);
    } else {
      console.log('Geocode was unsuccessful for the following reason:', status);
    }
  });
}
```

This script assumes that your data range is in columns A to C, rows 1 to 10. You can modify the `dataRange` variable to suit your specific needs.

To run the script, follow these steps:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script."
3. Copy and paste the script into the code editor.
4. Save the script by clicking on the floppy disk icon in the top left corner.
5. To run the script, click on the play button (▿) in the top left corner.

The script will search for the place name in the first cell of column A and store the latitude and longitude in the corresponding cells of columns C and D.

Here's a PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        return $order === 'asc' ? $a['age'] - $b['age'] : $b['age'] - $a['age'];
    });
    return $set;
}
